before:
```typescript
const example = { title: "Desk", copies: 2 };
type PrintJob = typeof example;
function jobText(job: PrintJob): string {
  return job.title + ": " + job.copies;
}
console.log(jobText({ title: "Map", copies: 5 }));
```
This prints Map: 5. jobText accepts PrintJob and returns a string made from title and copies. type PrintJob = typeof example derives the shape without creating or copying another object. Because the ordinary object fields widen to string and number, changed field values still fit. console.log displays the formatted result.

```typescript
const example = { title: "Desk", copies: 2 };
type PrintJob = typeof example;
function jobText(job: PrintJob): string {
  return job.title + ": " + job.copies;
}
console.log(jobText({ title: "Card", copies: 0 }));
```
This prints Card: 0. Trace the changed value through the same rule.

after:
```typescript
const example = { title: "Desk", copies: 2 };
type PrintJob = typeof example;
function jobText(job: PrintJob): string {
  return job.title + ": " + job.copies;
}
console.log(jobText({ title: "Map", copies: 5 }));
```
This prints `Map: 5`. `PrintJob` requires a text `title` and a number of `copies`.
